---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const APP = "web/app/src";

const H = {
  short: "How a page reads, and why it holds still",
  how: "The query client and the page's lifetime",
  rules: "The lint rules on reads",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Every read goes through TanStack Query and the generated client. Each read carries its
      query's signal, so TanStack can cancel it, and the document's lifetime, so leaving the page
      stops it on the wire; a write keeps its own lifetime and finishes. Two ESLint rules make
      both hold for every query. How pages also hold still while they load is on <PageLink
        to="Pages that hold still"
      />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      The app's query client keeps a read fresh for 30 seconds and retries a failed one twice,
      never one the API refused with a status below 500. It's a subclass, <code>PageQueryClient</code>,
      because a loader awaiting a read that a navigation cancelled should read again rather than fail
      with the cancellation (<Path p={`${APP}/api.ts`} sym="class PageQueryClient extends QueryClient" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      The client sends every <code>GET</code> and <code>HEAD</code> through <code>fetchPageRead</code>,
      which joins the caller's signal to the document's lifetime (<Path
        p={`${APP}/page-reads.ts`}
        sym="export const fetchPageRead"
      />). Reads stop at <code>beforeunload</code>, since browsers cut a leaving page's loads off
      anyway, and WebKit reports each as an access-control failure. If the navigation doesn't
      happen (a download, a 204, the person choosing to stay), the next press or key resumes them;
      a page restored from the back-forward cache resumes them too. Writes go straight to the
      browser's <code>fetch</code>, so a save in flight finishes after the page has gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Requests made while the person is idle carry a header that says so, so they don't keep the
      session open (<Adr n="0153" />). When a read answers 401, the app sends the person to sign in
      and back; a query marked as background instead marks the page signed out. Live
      updates arrive as events that invalidate queries, which then read again: <PageLink
        to="Tasks and live updates"
      /> covers the stream.
    </p>

  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Reads that can be cancelled and stop with the page" open>
        <p>Two rules in the repository's ESLint config, besides jsx-a11y strict and React's hooks rules:</p>
        <Excerpt file="eslint.config.js" start="^const QUERY_SIGNAL = \{" />
        <Excerpt file="eslint.config.js" start="^const POST_READ = \{" />
        <p>
          A third, <code>RAW_HTML</code>, bans <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>, which Trusted
          Types would refuse anyway (<PageLink to="Serving and security policy" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={`${APP}/api.ts`} />: the client and the query defaults</li>
        <li><Path p={`${APP}/page-reads.ts`} />: the page's lifetime in every read</li>
        <li><Path p="eslint.config.js" />: the rules on reads</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0029" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0153" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
